    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is the most definitive, primary indication for the insertion of a standard surgical tube thoracostomy (chest tube)?', '["A 10% asymptomatic primary spontaneous pneumothorax", "A large traumatic hemothorax or tension pneumothorax", "A simple rib fracture", "Pulmonary contusion"]', 1, 'A large hemothorax or tension pneumothorax requires immediate evacuation of blood/air to restore cardiopulmonary hemodynamics and re-expand the lung.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The "Triangle of Safety" is the anatomically preferred zone for inserting a chest tube. Which muscle forms the anterior border of this triangle?', '["Latissimus dorsi", "Pectoralis major", "Serratus anterior", "Rectus abdominis"]', 1, 'The triangle of safety is bordered by the lateral edge of the pectoralis major (anteriorly), the lateral edge of the latissimus dorsi (posteriorly), the base of the axilla (superiorly), and the horizontal line at the nipple level / 5th intercostal space (inferiorly).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Within the Triangle of Safety, the standard insertion site for a chest tube is the:', '["2nd or 3rd intercostal space, mid-clavicular line", "4th or 5th intercostal space, anterior or mid-axillary line", "7th or 8th intercostal space, posterior axillary line", "Subxiphoid approach"]', 1, 'The 4th or 5th ICS in the anterior/mid-axillary line avoids major muscles, breast tissue, and the diaphragm, which arches surprisingly high during expiration.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When making the incision and dissecting through the chest wall to insert a chest tube, the instrument MUST be passed:', '["Directly through the middle of the intercostal space", "Directly under (inferior to) the rib", "Directly over (superior to) the rib", "Through the cartilage of the rib"]', 2, 'The neurovascular bundle (intercostal vein, artery, and nerve) runs in the costal groove on the INFERIOR margin of every rib. Passing over the top of the rib avoids tearing these vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most likely severe complication if a chest tube is inadvertently forced under the inferior margin of a rib?', '["Pneumothorax", "Massive hemorrhage from a lacerated intercostal artery", "Chylothorax", "Esophageal perforation"]', 1, 'Intercostal arteries are under high systemic pressure. Lacerating one during chest tube placement can cause a life-threatening iatrogenic hemothorax.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which size chest tube is generally recommended for the evacuation of a massive traumatic hemothorax to prevent clotting within the tube?', '["8 to 14 French (Pigtail catheter)", "16 to 22 French", "24 to 28 French", "36 to 40 French (Large bore)"]', 3, 'Blood clots quickly. A large-bore tube (36-40F) is necessary to evacuate thick blood and clots. Smaller tubes will occlude and fail.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'For a simple, spontaneous pneumothorax (air only, no blood), which size chest tube is currently recommended by most guidelines?', '["8 to 14 French (e.g., Pigtail catheter)", "36 to 40 French", "45 French", "Large bore tubes are always required"]', 0, 'Air passes easily through small tubes. Small-bore pigtail catheters (8-14F) are just as effective for simple pneumothoraces and cause significantly less pain than large-bore surgical tubes.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Prior to making the incision for a chest tube, local anesthetic (e.g., 1% Lidocaine) should be infiltrated. Which anatomical layer is the most sensitive and requires the most thorough anesthesia?', '["The epidermis", "The subcutaneous fat", "The intercostal muscles", "The parietal pleura"]', 3, 'The parietal pleura is exquisitely sensitive (innervated by somatic pain fibers from the intercostal nerves). Copious anesthetic must be injected down to and including the pleura.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a surgical tube thoracostomy, what instrument is traditionally used to bluntly dissect through the intercostal muscles and puncture the parietal pleura?', '["A scalpel (#11 blade)", "A large Kelly clamp or Rochester-Pean forceps", "The chest tube itself", "A trocar"]', 1, 'A large, curved Kelly clamp is used to spread the muscle fibers bluntly (rather than cutting them) and pop through the pleura, minimizing bleeding and avoiding injury to the lung.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Immediately after puncturing the pleura with the Kelly clamp, the physician inserts a sterile, gloved finger into the pleural space. What is the primary purpose of this "finger sweep"?', '["To measure the size of the ribs", "To confirm entry into the pleural space, feel for the lung, and clear any adhesions that could prevent tube passage", "To stop bleeding from the intercostal artery", "To pull out blood clots manually"]', 1, 'The finger sweep confirms the tract is actually in the chest cavity (not in the subcutaneous tissue or abdomen) and breaks up delicate pleural adhesions that could tether the lung and cause the tube to lacerate it.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When inserting a chest tube specifically to drain a large pneumothorax, in which direction should the tip of the tube ideally be directed?', '["Inferiorly and posteriorly", "Apically (superiorly) and anteriorly", "Directly medially toward the heart", "Down into the costodiaphragmatic recess"]', 1, 'Air rises. Directing the tube to the apex of the lung ensures optimal evacuation of the pneumothorax.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When inserting a chest tube specifically to drain a hemothorax or pleural effusion, in which direction should the tip of the tube ideally be directed?', '["Inferiorly and posteriorly", "Apically (superiorly) and anteriorly", "Directly superiorly into the pleural dome", "Towards the axilla"]', 0, 'Fluid depends on gravity. Directing the tube inferiorly and posteriorly into the dependent portions of the chest (costodiaphragmatic recess) ensures optimal fluid drainage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A traditional 3-chamber chest drainage system (e.g., Pleur-evac) contains a collection chamber, a water seal chamber, and a suction control chamber. What is the fundamental purpose of the water seal?', '["To wash the blood as it drains", "To humidify the oxygen in the chest", "To act as a one-way valve, allowing air to escape the chest but preventing room air from being sucked back in during inspiration", "To measure the patient''s tidal volume"]', 2, 'The water seal creates a hydrostatic barrier. Air from the lung bubbles out through the water, but negative intrathoracic pressure cannot pull water (or air) back up the tube.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'While observing the water seal chamber of a patient with a newly placed chest tube for a pneumothorax, you notice continuous, vigorous bubbling. What does this indicate?', '["The lung is fully re-expanded", "There is an active air leak from the lung (bronchopleural fistula) or a leak in the external tubing", "The suction is turned up too high", "The chest tube is clogged"]', 1, 'Bubbling in the water seal chamber means air is actively passing from the patient''s chest into the system (an air leak).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'You observe the water level in the water seal chamber fluctuating up and down in sync with the patient''s spontaneous breathing. This phenomenon is known as:', '["Bubbling", "Tidaling", "Chugging", "Fistula tracking"]', 1, 'Tidaling reflects the normal transmission of negative and positive intrathoracic pressures during the respiratory cycle down the chest tube. It confirms the tube is patent and in the pleural space.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient''s chest tube has been draining a pneumothorax for 3 days. The nurse notes that "tidaling" in the water seal chamber has completely stopped. There is no bubbling. What is the most likely positive clinical reason for this?', '["The tube is kinked", "The lung has fully re-expanded and the pleural space is sealed", "The patient has stopped breathing", "The water seal is empty"]', 1, 'When the lung fully re-expands, the visceral pleura occludes the eyelets of the chest tube, stopping the transmission of respiratory pressure fluctuations (tidaling ceases).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the suction control chamber of a traditional wet-suction chest drain, the amount of negative pressure (suction) applied to the pleural space is determined by:', '["The dial on the wall vacuum regulator", "The diameter of the chest tube", "The height of the water column in the suction control chamber (usually set to -20 cm H2O)", "The patient''s blood pressure"]', 2, 'The wall suction must be turned up just enough to create gentle bubbling in the suction control chamber. The actual negative pressure transmitted to the patient is dictated solely by the depth of the water (usually 20 cm).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A trauma patient arrives with severe respiratory distress, absent breath sounds on the right, distended neck veins, and hypotension (BP 70/40). What is the most appropriate FIRST step?', '["Obtain a portable chest X-ray", "Perform a surgical tube thoracostomy immediately", "Perform an immediate needle decompression (thoracostomy) before placing the chest tube", "Intubate the patient"]', 2, 'This is a tension pneumothorax causing obstructive shock. Immediate needle decompression (or finger thoracostomy) is required to release the pressure instantly before taking the time to insert a formal chest tube.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Where are the two anatomically approved sites for performing an emergency needle decompression for a tension pneumothorax?', '["1st ICS mid-clavicular line OR 3rd ICS mid-axillary line", "2nd ICS mid-clavicular line OR 4th/5th ICS anterior axillary line", "6th ICS posterior axillary line OR subxiphoid", "Directly through the sternum"]', 1, 'Current ATLS guidelines recommend the 4th/5th ICS anterior axillary line as it has a lower failure rate due to thinner chest wall tissue, but the 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line remains an alternative.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A chest tube is placed for a traumatic hemothorax. Immediately upon insertion, 1,600 mL of blood drains rapidly into the collection chamber. What is the next most appropriate management step?', '["Clamp the tube to prevent further blood loss", "Administer tPA into the tube", "Prepare the patient for an emergency exploratory thoracotomy in the OR", "Transfuse 1 unit of blood and observe"]', 2, 'An initial output of >1,500 mL (or continuous bleeding of >200 mL/hr for 2-4 hours) defines a massive hemothorax indicating major vessel injury, requiring surgical repair (thoracotomy).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a massive, chronic pleural effusion (3 Liters) has a chest tube placed. The physician decides to clamp the tube after draining 1.5 Liters and drain the rest slowly over the next 24 hours. Why?', '["To prevent hypovolemic shock", "To prevent Re-expansion Pulmonary Edema (REPE)", "To keep the tube from clogging", "To allow antibiotics to work"]', 1, 'Rapidly expanding a lung that has been collapsed for days can cause severe reperfusion injury and capillary leak, leading to life-threatening unilateral re-expansion pulmonary edema. Drainage is typically capped at 1-1.5L at a time.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'After placing a chest tube, the patient develops severe swelling of the chest wall, neck, and face. The skin feels like "bubble wrap" when palpated. What is this condition called, and what does it indicate regarding the chest tube?', '["Myxedema; indicates thyroid storm", "Subcutaneous emphysema; suggests the fenestration (eyelet) of the tube is outside the pleural space or there is an inadequate seal at the skin", "Anasarca; indicates heart failure", "Angioedema; indicates an allergic reaction"]', 1, 'If the most proximal hole on the chest tube is not fully inside the chest cavity, air from the lung will be blown directly into the subcutaneous tissues of the chest wall.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When securing a chest tube, a heavy, non-absorbable suture (e.g., 0 or 1-0 Silk) is used. Which technique is commonly employed to both anchor the tube and allow for immediate closure of the tract upon removal?', '["A simple interrupted stitch", "A continuous running stitch", "A purse-string suture or ''Roman sandal'' technique", "A figure-of-eight stitch"]', 2, 'The suture is tied tightly around the tube (often laced like a sandal) to prevent it from slipping out. The long ends are left intact so they can be tied immediately as a purse-string when the tube is pulled.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a standard clinical criterion for the safe removal of a chest tube placed for a pneumothorax?', '["The patient requests removal", "The lung is fully re-expanded on CXR, and there has been no bubbling (air leak) in the water seal for at least 24 hours", "The tube has been in for exactly 48 hours", "The patient has a fever"]', 1, 'Resolution of the air leak (no bubbling on water seal or provocative cough test) and radiologic evidence of lung expansion dictate when the tube can come out.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'To minimize the risk of sucking air back into the pleural space (causing a recurrent pneumothorax) during chest tube removal, the patient should be instructed to:', '["Breathe normally", "Take a deep breath in, and perform a Valsalva maneuver (bear down) while the tube is rapidly pulled", "Exhale completely and pant like a dog", "Cough forcefully continuously"]', 1, 'Valsalva (or exhaling completely and holding it) increases intrathoracic pressure above atmospheric pressure. This ensures that when the tube is pulled, air is pushed out of the tract rather than sucked in.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A chest tube is inadvertently inserted at the 8th intercostal space in the mid-axillary line on the right side. Which intra-abdominal organ is at highest risk of being lacerated by the clamp or the tube?', '["The stomach", "The spleen", "The liver", "The kidney"]', 2, 'The diaphragm rises as high as the 4th/5th ICS during expiration. Inserting a tube too low on the right side frequently results in diaphragmatic perforation and liver laceration.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A nurse notices that the tubing connecting the patient''s chest tube to the Pleur-evac has become disconnected and fallen on the floor. The patient is in respiratory distress. What is the immediate first action?', '["Clamp the chest tube near the patient''s skin with a padded hemostat", "Submerge the exposed end of the chest tube into a bottle of sterile water or saline", "Push the tube further into the chest", "Order a stat chest X-ray"]', 1, 'Submerging the tube creates a temporary, makeshift water seal. Clamping the tube is dangerous because if the patient has an active air leak, it will immediately cause a tension pneumothorax.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is it strictly taught NEVER to leave a chest tube clamped for transport or ambulation?', '["It causes the blood to clot in the tube", "If the patient has a bronchopleural air leak, clamping the tube converts a simple pneumothorax into a lethal tension pneumothorax", "It causes severe pain", "It causes the lung to collapse immediately"]', 1, 'A clamped tube cannot vent air. If air is entering the pleura from a lung injury, the pressure will rapidly build until the heart is compressed (tension pneumothorax).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with an open pneumothorax ("sucking chest wound") from a stabbing is treated by EMS with a 3-sided occlusive dressing. Upon arrival at the ED, what is the definitive management?', '["Suture the wound closed tightly", "Remove the dressing and leave the wound open", "Place a formal chest tube at a separate, intact site on the chest wall, then close the stab wound", "Intubate the patient and ignore the wound"]', 2, 'The 3-sided dressing is a temporary flutter valve. Definitive treatment requires a chest tube to re-expand the lung, placed away from the contaminated injury site, followed by surgical closure of the defect.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with a traumatic hemothorax. A chest tube is placed, but the X-ray shows a large amount of retained, clotted blood in the pleural space. Why must this retained hemothorax be aggressively managed (e.g., with VATS or fibrinolytics)?', '["To prevent the blood from turning into air", "Retained blood acts as a culture medium for empyema and causes fibrothorax (scarring that traps the lung)", "To prevent deep vein thrombosis", "To allow the ribs to heal"]', 1, 'Undrained blood in the pleural cavity inevitably organizes into a fibrous peel that permanently constricts the lung ("trapped lung") and has a very high risk of becoming infected (empyema).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following pleural fluid laboratory results diagnostic of an Empyema mandates immediate chest tube drainage?', '["pH 7.45", "Glucose 100 mg/dL", "Frank pus, positive Gram stain, or a pleural fluid pH < 7.20", "Protein 2.5 g/dL"]', 2, 'Pus, bacteria, or a highly acidic environment (pH < 7.2) indicates a severe, complicated parapneumonic effusion or empyema that will not resolve with antibiotics alone; it must be drained.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A tall, thin 18-year-old male with no medical history presents with sudden onset sharp right-sided chest pain and shortness of breath. CXR shows a 30% right pneumothorax. What is the most likely pathophysiology?', '["Rupture of a subpleural apical bleb (Primary Spontaneous Pneumothorax)", "Rupture of a COPD bulla (Secondary Spontaneous Pneumothorax)", "Pulmonary embolism", "Costochondritis"]', 0, 'Primary spontaneous pneumothorax typically occurs in tall, thin, young men due to the rupture of small congenital blebs at the apex of the lung.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old smoker with severe COPD develops a pneumothorax. This is classified as a Secondary Spontaneous Pneumothorax. How does the management generally differ from a primary spontaneous pneumothorax?', '["It can usually be observed without a chest tube", "It is much more dangerous, almost always requires a chest tube, and mandates hospital admission", "It is treated with antibiotics only", "It requires an immediate pneumonectomy"]', 1, 'Patients with underlying lung disease (COPD, CF) have poor baseline reserve. A pneumothorax is life-threatening and almost universally requires intervention (chest tube) and admission.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a known history of advanced heart failure presents with a large right-sided pleural effusion. Thoracentesis is performed. According to Light''s Criteria, which finding confirms the fluid is a TRANSUDATE?', '["Pleural fluid to serum protein ratio > 0.5", "Pleural fluid to serum LDH ratio > 0.6", "Pleural fluid LDH > 2/3 the upper limit of normal serum LDH", "None of the above; transudates have low protein and low LDH"]', 3, 'Light''s criteria define an EXUDATE (high protein, high LDH). Heart failure causes increased hydrostatic pressure, leading to a transudate (watery fluid low in protein and LDH).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Light''s criteria are used to determine if a pleural effusion is an exudate. Which of the following conditions classically produces an EXUDATIVE effusion?', '["Cirrhosis", "Nephrotic syndrome", "Congestive Heart Failure", "Pneumonia (Parapneumonic effusion) or Malignancy"]', 3, 'Infections and cancers cause pleural inflammation and increased capillary permeability, leaking protein and LDH into the pleural space (Exudate).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 30-year-old female presents with recurrent right-sided pneumothoraces that mysteriously occur exactly 48 hours after the onset of her menstrual cycle every month. What is this condition called?', '["Tension pneumothorax", "Catamenial pneumothorax", "Traumatic pneumothorax", "Primary spontaneous pneumothorax"]', 1, 'Catamenial pneumothorax is caused by thoracic endometriosis. Endometrial implants on the pleura or diaphragm bleed and slough during menses, creating holes that let air escape into the chest.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient has a thick, loculated empyema that is not draining well through a properly placed chest tube. To avoid invasive surgery (VATS), what pharmacological therapy can be instilled directly into the chest tube to break up the loculations?', '["Intrapleural tPA (Alteplase) and DNase (Dornase alfa)", "Intrapleural Heparin", "Intrapleural Corticosteroids", "Intrapleural Epinephrine"]', 0, 'The MIST-2 trial showed that the combination of tPA (fibrinolytic) and DNase (breaks down thick DNA in pus) effectively liquefies thick empyemas, allowing them to drain and often preventing the need for surgery.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following describes the correct use of a Heimlich valve?', '["It is a valve used to intubate the trachea", "It is a one-way flutter valve attached to a chest tube that allows air to escape but not enter, allowing the patient to be mobile without a bulky water-seal chamber", "It is used to stop bleeding in the chest", "It is a valve in the heart"]', 1, 'A Heimlich valve is a portable, dry, one-way valve. It is ideal for patients with a persistent simple pneumothorax who are stable enough to ambulate or be discharged home.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During chest tube insertion, the use of a trocar (a sharp, pointed metal rod inserted inside the tube to puncture the chest wall) is currently:', '["The gold standard and highly recommended", "Recommended only for pediatric patients", "Strongly discouraged due to a very high risk of lung laceration and intrathoracic organ injury", "Used only for hemothoraces"]', 2, 'Blindly shoving a sharp trocar into the chest frequently results in puncturing the lung, heart, or great vessels. Blunt dissection with a Kelly clamp and finger sweep is the safe standard of care.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient arrives with a traumatic flail chest (paradoxical inward movement of a chest wall segment during inspiration). They are hypoxic. What is the definitive management of the airway/chest mechanics if they cannot maintain oxygenation?', '["Wrap the chest tightly with bandages", "Place a chest tube immediately", "Intubation and positive pressure mechanical ventilation (which splints the chest wall internally)", "Needle decompression"]', 2, 'Positive pressure ventilation pushes the flail segment outward during inspiration, correcting the paradoxical motion ("internal pneumatic splinting") and allowing the underlying pulmonary contusion to heal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When evaluating a post-chest tube insertion chest X-ray, the "radiopaque stripe" on the chest tube is used to verify:', '["That the tube is not infected", "That the tube is made of silicone", "That all the drainage holes (eyelets), marked by the break in the stripe, are fully within the pleural cavity", "The size of the tube"]', 2, 'The radiopaque line runs the length of the tube and is interrupted at the most proximal drainage hole. If this break in the line is outside the rib cage, the hole is in the soft tissue, causing subcutaneous emphysema.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is stabbed in the left lower chest (8th ICS). A chest tube is placed, and initially returns 500 mL of blood, but then you notice dark brown fluid with a foul odor draining from the tube. What injury must be highly suspected?', '["Aortic rupture", "Esophageal or Gastric perforation with diaphragmatic injury", "Tracheal laceration", "Spleen laceration"]', 1, 'Dark brown, foul-smelling fluid or food particles draining from a chest tube strongly indicates the knife passed through the diaphragm into the stomach or bowel, creating a fistula.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a potential complication of applying high wall suction (e.g., -40 cm H2O) to a chest tube system in a patient with a fresh, healing pneumothorax?', '["It cures the pneumothorax instantly", "It pulls the lung tissue into the eyelets of the tube, causing pain and maintaining the air leak by preventing the visceral pleura from healing", "It causes the tube to fall out", "It prevents fluid drainage"]', 1, 'Excessive suction can literally suck the delicate lung tissue into the tube holes, keeping the fistula open. Suction is usually kept at -20 cm H2O and often transitioned to "water seal only" to allow the leak to heal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a massive hemothorax is bleeding heavily. The trauma surgeon decides to perform an "autotransfusion." What does this involve?', '["Transfusing type O negative blood", "Collecting the sterile blood draining from the chest tube into a specialized canister and transfusing it back into the patient''s IV", "Giving the patient fresh frozen plasma", "Using a cell-saver during a laparotomy"]', 1, 'Blood shed into the pleural space is relatively sterile and defibrinated (doesn''t clot easily). It can be collected in an autotransfusion system and safely given back to the hemorrhaging patient, saving blood bank resources.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'If a patient requires a chest tube for a massive malignant pleural effusion to provide symptomatic relief, but the effusion rapidly reaccumulates, what definitive procedure is often performed through the chest tube?', '["Pleurodesis (instillation of a sclerosing agent like talc or doxycycline to fuse the pleural layers)", "Pneumonectomy", "Instillation of tPA", "Instillation of antibiotics"]', 0, 'Pleurodesis creates severe inflammation that scars the visceral and parietal pleura together, obliterating the pleural space so fluid can no longer accumulate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When performing a diagnostic thoracentesis (needle drainage of pleural fluid) to determine the cause of an effusion, what is the maximum amount of fluid that should typically be removed at one time to prevent re-expansion pulmonary edema?', '["100 mL", "500 mL", "1500 mL", "3000 mL"]', 2, 'Draining more than 1.5 Liters at a single time significantly increases the risk of re-expansion pulmonary edema and post-procedure hypotension.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a chest tube is being transported to CT. The transporter asks if they should clamp the tube so the fluid doesn''t spill. The correct instruction is:', '["Yes, clamp it securely", "No, never clamp it; keep the Pleur-evac upright and below the level of the patient''s chest", "Clamp it only if the patient coughs", "Disconnect the tube from the Pleur-evac entirely"]', 1, 'The drainage system must always remain below the level of the chest to prevent fluid from draining back into the patient, and it must NEVER be clamped during transport due to the risk of tension pneumothorax.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following findings on a supine AP chest X-ray in a trauma patient strongly suggests a pneumothorax, even if a classic apical pleural line is not visible?', '["The deep sulcus sign (a very deep, dark costophrenic angle on the affected side)", "Blunting of the costophrenic angle", "A widened mediastinum", "Kerley B lines"]', 0, 'In a supine patient, air rises to the highest point, which is the anterior, inferior base of the lung. This forces the costophrenic angle down, creating a sharp, hyperlucent "deep sulcus."'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is intubated and on positive pressure ventilation. They develop a simple 15% pneumothorax. What is the mandatory next step?', '["Observation and repeat CXR in 4 hours", "Immediate placement of a chest tube or pigtail catheter", "Administer 100% oxygen and wait", "Extubate the patient"]', 1, 'A simple pneumothorax in a patient on a mechanical ventilator will almost universally be converted into a lethal tension pneumothorax by the positive pressure pushing air through the fistula. It MUST be drained.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary difference between a transudate and an exudate regarding pleural fluid pathophysiology?', '["Transudates are caused by increased capillary permeability (inflammation); exudates are caused by altered hydrostatic/oncotic pressure", "Transudates are caused by altered hydrostatic/oncotic pressure (e.g., heart failure); exudates are caused by increased capillary permeability (e.g., inflammation/infection)", "Transudates are always bloody; exudates are clear", "Transudates have high protein; exudates have low protein"]', 1, 'Transudates are a mechanical pressure problem (plumbing). Exudates are an inflammatory or malignant problem causing capillary leak.');
